winebus: let a DualSense be driven by XInput without taking anything away from it.

A game that reads XInput gets no vibration from a DualSense under wine.
xinput1_3 drives a pad through a HID haptics collection (usage page 0x0E)
and nothing else, and no report descriptor Sony ships contains one. wine can
build that collection -- hid_device_add_haptics does it for every pad winebus
describes itself -- but a DualSense is not one of those: it arrives with its
own descriptor, which is precisely what makes the adaptive triggers, the
touchpad and Sony's own library work. Replacing it is what the SDL bus does,
and the price is all three.

So the collection is APPENDED. The descriptor is left byte for byte as it was
and two collections are added after it, rumble and buzz, sharing one new
feature id and one new output id. Both new reports are far shorter than what
a DualSense already declares, so the three report lengths libScePad gates on
do not move -- asserted for the plain pad and for both of the Edge captured
descriptors by dualsense-descriptors/haptics_augment.py, which parses the
bytes this build really produces rather than a copy of them.

Off unless the device key says XInputRumble. In this step an intensity
written to the collection is READ AND TRACED, not acted on: the question this
answers first is whether offering the collection costs a pad anything it
already did, and moving the motors as well would answer two questions at once
and tell us which one failed.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix

AND THE SECOND HALF, for Bluetooth. Over there the pad sends report 0x31, and its
own descriptor declares all 77 bytes of it as one vendor block -- not an axis,
not a button. The nine-byte report 0x01 that does declare them is the one the pad
STOPS SENDING as soon as anything reads a feature report from it, which
everything does. So over Bluetooth nothing that goes through HidP -- xinput,
dinput, any of it -- can read the pad's state at all.

The fields are there; only the declaration was missing. This replaces that opaque
block with Sony's own declaration of the SAME payload from the pad's USB
descriptor, copied item for item, with one byte of padding in front for the
sequence byte Bluetooth adds. And then it takes the state declaration off report
0x01, which is not sent any more: two reports claiming to carry the device's
state made dinput say "multiple device state reports found!" twenty-two times in
one session, and which one it reads is not a thing to leave to chance. The
report keeps its nine bytes; they are simply no longer named.
