@@ -0,0 +1,221 @@
+/*
+ * MGVF mgvf-0010: HID haptics on a pad that keeps its own report descriptor.
+ *
+ * A game that reads XInput gets no vibration from a DualSense here. wine's
+ * xinput1_3 does know how to rumble a pad, but only through a HID haptics
+ * collection (usage page 0x0E): it looks for a Waveform List, reads which
+ * waveform each one drives, and writes an Intensity value to the output report
+ * beside it. winebus already builds exactly that collection for the pads it
+ * describes itself, in hid_device_add_haptics -- but a DualSense is not one of
+ * those. It arrives with its own report descriptor, which winebus passes
+ * through untouched, and no descriptor Sony ships contains a haptics page.
+ *
+ * So the collection is appended to the pad's real descriptor instead of
+ * replacing it. Everything the pad already declares stays exactly where it was
+ * -- the adaptive triggers, the touchpad, the gyro, the feature reports Sony's
+ * own library reads -- and two collections are added after it, one for rumble
+ * and one for buzz, sharing one new feature report id and one new output id.
+ *
+ * The three numbers libScePad gates on are the *longest* report of each kind,
+ * and both new reports are far shorter than what a DualSense already declares,
+ * so none of them moves. That is the whole reason this can be an addition
+ * rather than a replacement, and dualsense-descriptors/haptics_augment.py
+ * asserts it against the descriptors captured from the real pads.
+ *
+ * This file is the pure half: descriptor bytes, the feature report's contents,
+ * and the reading of an output report. It has no kernel or wine dependency so
+ * that the host test can compile it. main.c decides when to call these and
+ * turns the intensities into motor levels.
